{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C1GiwYLS18KQeWwZr433oB","type":"tool_result","content":"141-720. Omen Cards\n142-721. Station Cards\n143:722. Preparation Cards\n144-723. Controlling Another Player\n145-724. Ending Turns and Phases\n146-725. The Monarch\n147-726. The Initiative\n148-727. Restarting the Game\n149-728. Rad Counters\n150-729. Subgames\n151-730. Merging with Permanents\n152-731. Day and Night\n153-732. Taking Shortcuts\n154-733. Handling Illegal Actions\n155-\n156-8. Multiplayer Rules\n157-800. General\n158-801. Limited Range of Influence Option\n159-802. Attack Multiple Players Option\n160-803. Attack Left and Attack Right Options\n161-804. Deploy Creatures Option\n162-805. Shared Team Turns Option\n163-806. Free-for-All Variant\n164-807. Grand Melee Variant\n165-808. Team vs. Team Variant\n166-809. Emperor Variant\n167-810. Two-Headed Giant Variant\n168-811. Alternating Teams Variant\n169-\n170-9. Casual Variants\n171-900. General\n172-901. Planechase\n173-902. Vanguard\n174-903. Commander\n175-904. Archenemy\n176-905. Conspiracy Draft\n177-\n178-Glossary\n179-\n180-Credits\n181-\n182-1. Game Concepts\n183-\n--\n6098-721.4. Any ability a station card has that isn’t preceded by a station symbol is treated normally. In particular, each station card has its station ability (see rule 702.184) at all times. That ability may be activated regardless of how many charge counters are on it.\n6099-\n6100:722. Preparation Cards\n6101-\n6102:722.1. Preparation cards have a two-part card frame, with a smaller frame inset within their text box.\n6103-\n6104:722.2. The text that appears in the inset frame on the right defines alternative characteristics that the object may have while it’s a spell. The card’s normal characteristics appear as usual, although with a smaller text box on the left.\n6105-\n6106:722.2a The inset frame of a preparation card is called a “prepare spell.” If a rule or effect refers to a card, spell, or permanent that has a prepare spell, it refers to an object for which these alternative characteristics exist, even if the object currently doesn’t use them.\n6107-\n6108:722.2b The existence and values of these alternative characteristics are part of the object’s copiable values.\n6109-\n6110:722.2c Although preparation cards are printed with multiple sets of characteristics, each preparation card is only one card. For example, a player who has drawn or discarded a preparation card has drawn or discarded one card, not two.\n6111-\n6112:722.3. Preparation cards can’t be cast using the alternative characteristics found within their inset frames. Rather, these characteristics are used to define characteristics of copies which may be cast.\n6113-\n6114:722.3a Some spells and abilities cause a permanent with a prepare spell to become prepared or state that a permanent enters prepared. If that permanent has the alternative characteristics of a prepare spell, this gives the permanent the “prepared” designation. Prepared is a designation that acts as a marker which rules and effects can identify. A permanent can’t gain this designation unless it has a prepare spell, Additionally, a permanent can’t gain this designation if the permanent already has it.\n6115-\n6116:722.3b A rule or effect may cause a permanent to become “unprepared.” This removes the prepared designation from that permanent.\n6117-\n6118:722.3c As a permanent with a prepare spell gains the prepared designation or phases in prepared, its controller creates a copy of that object in exile, except that copy has only the characteristics of that permanent’s prepare spell, ignoring other exceptions to the copying process that apply to that permanent. Those characteristics become the copy’s normal characteristics. This copy remains in exile for as long as the prepared permanent remains on the battlefield and has the prepared designation. This is an exception to rule 704.5e. For as long as the copy remains in exile, the prepared permanent’s controller may cast the copy. That permanent loses the prepared designation at the time the spell becomes cast (see rule 601.2i).\n6119-Example: A player casts Croaking Counterpart targeting an Encouraging Aviator. Croaking Counterpart is an instant that says, in part, “Create a token that’s a copy of target non-Frog creature, except it’s a 1/1 green Frog.” Encouraging Aviator is a preparation creature card which says, in part, “Whenever this creature attacks, it becomes prepared.” Its prepare spell is an instant named Jump which says “Target creature gains flying until end of turn.” The player then attacks with the token copy of Encouraging Aviator. As the token copy of Encouraging Aviator becomes prepared, the copy created in exile is a blue instant named Jump that says “Target creature gains flying until end of turn.” It is not green, nor does it have power and toughness 1/1 or the Frog creature type.\n6120-\n6121:722.3d If a prepare spell is copied, the copy is also a prepare spell. Any rule or effect that refers to a spell cast as a prepare spell refers to the copy as well.\n6122-\n6123:722.4. In every zone, a preparation card has only its normal characteristics.\n6124-\n6125:722.5. If an effect instructs a player to choose a card name and the player wants to choose a preparation card’s alternative name, the player may do so.\n6126-\n6127-723. Controlling Another Player\n6128-\n6129-723.1. Some cards allow a player to control another player during that player’s next turn. This effect applies to the next turn that the affected player actually takes. The affected player is controlled during the entire turn; the effect doesn’t end until the beginning of the next turn.\n6130-\n6131-723.1a Multiple player-controlling effects that affect the same player overwrite each other. The last one to be created is the one that works.\n6132-\n6133-723.1b If a turn is skipped, any pending player-controlling effects wait until the player who would be affected actually takes a turn.\n===\n\"\"\"Card implementation for Emeritus of Truce // Swords to Plowshares.\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom engine.card import CardImpl\n\nfrom typing import TYPE_CHECKING\n\nif TYPE_CHECKING:\n    from engine.game_state import GameState\n\n\nclass EmeritusOfTruceSwordsToPlowshares(CardImpl):\n    \"\"\"TODO: Implement Emeritus of Truce // Swords to Plowshares.\"\"\"\n\n    pass\n===","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"91030fde-ab39-43e5-99c8-7491eecaa6bd","uuid":"d1e7c5ff-8e1c-4b41-8baf-1a2798d7bf03","timestamp":"2026-06-10T17:05:09.031Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"141-720. Omen Cards\n142-721. Station Cards\n143:722. Preparation Cards\n144-723. Controlling Another Player\n145-724. Ending Turns and Phases\n146-725. The Monarch\n147-726. The Initiative\n148-727. Restarting the Game\n149-728. Rad Counters\n150-729. Subgames\n151-730. Merging with Permanents\n152-731. Day and Night\n153-732. Taking Shortcuts\n154-733. Handling Illegal Actions\n155-\n156-8. Multiplayer Rules\n157-800. General\n158-801. Limited Range of Influence Option\n159-802. Attack Multiple Players Option\n160-803. Attack Left and Attack Right Options\n161-804. Deploy Creatures Option\n162-805. Shared Team Turns Option\n163-806. Free-for-All Variant\n164-807. Grand Melee Variant\n165-808. Team vs. Team Variant\n166-809. Emperor Variant\n167-810. Two-Headed Giant Variant\n168-811. Alternating Teams Variant\n169-\n170-9. Casual Variants\n171-900. General\n172-901. Planechase\n173-902. Vanguard\n174-903. Commander\n175-904. Archenemy\n176-905. Conspiracy Draft\n177-\n178-Glossary\n179-\n180-Credits\n181-\n182-1. Game Concepts\n183-\n--\n6098-721.4. Any ability a station card has that isn’t preceded by a station symbol is treated normally. In particular, each station card has its station ability (see rule 702.184) at all times. That ability may be activated regardless of how many charge counters are on it.\n6099-\n6100:722. P